<p>Hi Kalinin,</p>
<p>First of all thanks a lot for the detailed feedback! In all honesty I did not expect such an in-depth review on the first day, especially not after having officially posted this. I posted this earlier in my sandbox and worked on it in the forums and because I received mostly positive feedback with stylistic discrepancies, I thought I am ready for submitting. I saw your reply when I was on train and I rushed back home to make the edits.</p>
<p>Here are my responses:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The &quot;SCP&quot; acronym thing appears a lot in older articles, but it makes absolutely no sense in-universe.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This was recommended to me by another member in the sandbox, and at the time I thought this was pretty smart and I guess I was a little high on life or something. After a sober review this does appear rather out-of-place and I have changed it to the original pseudonym.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In light of&quot; is a little informal for what you're going for here &#8230; in the master bedroom.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All points taken and changes have been made as I deemed fit in this part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is another thing that happens a lot (so it's not really limited to you) but doesn't make sense to me. Why would knowing when something happened within a ten-year period be okay, but not the particular year? Why wouldn't you just blackbox the whole year if the date is privileged information?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How I interpreted blackboxing is that it's to hide certain details, while still providing enough background for the reader to gain at least a vague sense of understanding. A lot could happen in 10 years and because the discovery also sets the time of the journal's earliest entry, I want to at least provide this much information to the reader.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is an extraneous sentence. &#8230; You can just say &quot;currently.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All points taken and changes have been made as I deemed fit in this part.</p>
<p>——————Changes made above this line——————</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Then it's not an experiment. Experiments are things that are designed and carried out purposefully to gather data under controlled conditions. While data can be gathered from accidental occurrences, it's poor terminology to refer to something like that as an experiment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The truth is I too debated with myself on this when I wrote it, but I do find more reasoning in your argument and I will change this to an incident report.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The &quot;god-forsaken&quot; bit is where the narm starts creeping into this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Will look into get rid of the narm problem within 24 hours. Already have a plan and I am contemplating the execution, but it's coming.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why is that redacted?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is completely my bad. Long story short: used blackbox instead of a marker when I wrote this and left it thinking I'll go back to edit it. It just escaped me when I worked through editing it for final draft.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sort of a weird observation for a middle aged Canadian white guy in the suburbs to make.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Henry's setting is an educated elite white collar in Vancouver, a very modernized Canadian city in the west coast. The site's location is a suburban area in the Greater Vancouver region. It's like Manhattan and Brooklyn.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So he's modern enough to have hung out with people who've worked on stuff like drones before, but he's still speaking about it like it's an esoteric particle collider or some such? Weird style here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The use and mentioning of drones was not popularized in North America until 2012, and considering this took place at least a decade earlier, and that Henry was involved in architecture not engineering, I would say his alienation is at least understandable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He's young enough to have had a small child when he went in, but he should be dead within seven years? Huh?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The addendum is a collection of excerpts, and I used &#8230; to indicate omission. In here he is talking about had he counted the years he spent in the SCP. I suppose this is just how we interpreted this differently?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If I'm to buy that this guy has been changed by his experiences of continually walking for redacted-hundreds of years, he's going to need to sound a little bit different than this. This is the same character with some dialogue that's clearly trying to sound world-weary and insightful, but not really succeeding.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've thought a lot about this when I wrote it. I came to the conclusion that without taking in new knowledge (as one would go about life in the ordinary world), the development of the character is limited to how he processes the knowledge he already has, which is why I went with the world-weary direction.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I don't think that's what &quot;impressionable&quot; means.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, a horrible choice of wording. Thanks for pointing it out.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I also looked into the plot summary of House of Leaves and found the resemblance you spoke of. And please believe me when I say that I have never read of the book (only heard of its title), and the concept of this SCP was from a nightmare I had some years ago.</p>
<p>Thanks again for your feedback. Please check back tomorrow if you have time. I understand what I'm doing is already beyond what the official SCP section is meant for, and I will do my best to present a more refined version fitting to be posted.</p>

							</div>

								
					
